  formatting is a presentation concern that belongs in [38;5;153mrender_text[39m and nowhere else. A [38;5;153mtotal[39m row in a CSV is the
  classic thing that poisons a spreadsheet import.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What shape should the CSV and JSON carry? → Integer cents (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Locked: integer cents, total as its own field, no total row in the CSV.

  Last question, then I'll write up the design.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how the cron line names the output file and picks the format. This is the part someone reads at 3am during
  an incident, so explicitness has real value.

  - [1mExplicit [38;5;153m--out PATH[39m + [38;5;153m--format csv|json[0m — [38;5;153mreportkit export --day 2026-09-11 --format csv --out [39m
    [38;5;153m/var/reports/billing-2026-09-11.csv[39m. Slightly redundant when the extension already says [38;5;153mcsv[39m, but the command states
    exactly what it does and there's no rule to remember. Adding extension inference later is purely additive.
  - [1mInfer format from the extension[0m — [38;5;153m--out …/billing.csv[39m is enough, [38;5;153m--format[39m only as an override. Shorter, but you owe
    an answer for [38;5;153m.txt[39m, no extension, and a [38;5;153m.csv[39m path the caller meant as JSON — and a wrong guess writes a silently
    mislabeled file.
  - [1m[38;5;153m--format[39m + [38;5;153m--out-dir[39m, filename derived[0m — reportkit owns the naming ([38;5;153mbilling-2026-09-11.csv[39m), so every night's file
    is consistent. Costs you control over the path, which matters if a downstream job expects a fixed name.

  On overwrite: I'd plainly overwrite an existing file in all three. Cron re-runs after a failure are normal, and a
  command that refuses the second run turns a transient blip into manual cleanup.
